  "problem": "Which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=\\frac{1}{8}(4-x)({x}^{2}+6)?$\n\n(Note: Figures not drawn to scale)",
  "result": "Correct",
  "worked_solution": "The equation is already fully factored. Note that the discriminant of ${x}^{2}+6=0$ is \n$D={0}^{2}-4(1)(6)=-24<0,$\nwhich implies that the quadratic factor has no real roots. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that the function $f(x)$ has a single root at $x=4.$\n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by plugging the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n$\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{8}(4-0)({0}^{2}+6) \\\\  & =\\frac{(4)(6)}{8} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}$\n\nSo, the curve:\n\nhas a $y$-intercept at the point $(0,3),$\n\npasses through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=4,$ and\n\nhas no more $x$-intercepts.\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the graph has an $S$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](https://mathacademy.com/graphics/q-28197-a-1)",
